        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 724,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 676,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Raises 2 to the power of every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is the natural exponential for anything measured in octaves or\ndoublings: a pitch n semitones up has frequency ratio 2 to the n/12, and a\nvalue that doubles every step grows as 2 to the step.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nAlways positive, rising faster and faster: 1 at 0, 2 at 1, 16 at 4, and\nhalving with each step left: 0.5 at -1, 0.0625 at -4. Whole-number inputs\ngive exact powers of 2.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.exp2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nOverflows to infinity above about 128 in 32-bit floats, and underflows to\n0 below about -149.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-4 to 4 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is 2 to the x of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -4 to 4,\ndrawn from 0 (bottom) to 16.5 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nexp raises e rather than 2; log2 undoes this. t.logspace makes a\nseries of evenly spaced powers directly.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 660,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Raises 2 to the power of every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is the natural exponential for anything measured in octaves or\ndoublings: a pitch n semitones up has frequency ratio 2 to the n/12, and a\nvalue that doubles every step grows as 2 to the step.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nAlways positive, rising faster and faster: 1 at 0, 2 at 1, 16 at 4, and\nhalving with each step left: 0.5 at -1, 0.0625 at -4. Whole-number inputs\ngive exact powers of 2.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.exp2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nOverflows to infinity above about 128 in 32-bit floats, and underflows to\n0 below about -149.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-4 to 4 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is 2 to the x of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -4 to 4,\ndrawn from 0 (bottom) to 16.5 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nexp raises e rather than 2; log2 undoes this. t.logspace makes a\nseries of evenly spaced powers directly."
        },
